{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head a4747ecf8b4ff14ca393c9a10a777ee5b69dc01c, tree 351291af026aeffaf6495a2661e16d553f65f230, on builder/e1-core-client-20261114, all 57 artifacts, for request c2bd5efc under implementation promise 14b911fd and my promise 360300a0. The E1 note `notes/implementation/2026-11-14-e1-explore.md` is the primary report. I read the head from the shared object store. The branch is not on origin.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- `packages/derive/src/judge.ts`, whole diff. `actJudged` keeps every envelope step: signature, digest, actor, `notAfter` and intent lifetime. It then calls `actBodyJudged` with a frame that holds the signed intent, the digest and the actor, and builds the same `input` from the returned authority, presented facts and retained observations. In that frame, `references`, `unavailableIntent`, `unavailableEntry`, `selfRefs`, `recordRefs` and `itemBookkeeping` are all absent. Each new branch in the body tests one of those members, so a recorded act takes the old path. The grant search still requires `grant.key` to equal the intent's actor. `judgesTime` stays true whenever the frame holds a signed intent. `judgeActBody` builds its frame without a signature, catches a reached need for the intent or for an entry symbol, and then answers `unavailable` with `lacks` and no partial effects. Its `write` answer carries no `input`, so it cannot become an entry.\n- `packages/client/src/expected.ts`, whole diff. `expectedOf`, `subjectsOf` and `viaLeft` become generic over the item address and the revision type. For numeric items, every branch answers as before: a `by` field that is not a number finds no item, as the old `typeof` test did; the new identity fallback in `viaId` matches only a non-numeric slot value, and `viaLeft` still lists only numeric IDs. The MCP shape test drops its cast and keeps its expected keys.\n- `packages/derive/src/capability.ts`, `effects.ts`, `operand.ts` and `program/body.ts`. `publicGiven` returns the given object unchanged unless `unavailableIntent` is set. `readSlotValue` returns `slotOf` when the judgment has no references. `executeEffects` creates its `changed` map only for item bookkeeping, so the new hold-end branch runs only in exploration. `withCapabilityRecords` keeps the record collector in one module variable for one synchronous call and restores it in `finally`. A guard runs with no collector, so nested runtime code inherits none. A record mark from any capability other than `device-command@1` refuses with `entry`.\n- `packages/derive/src/body-references.ts` and `opaque-object.ts`, and the client modules `act-explore.ts`, `act-projection.ts`, `act-references.ts` and `act-opaque.ts`. Exploration copies the view at its cut, refuses a second assumption of the same source, and reports a refusal with only its reason, class and name. Neither `packages/client/src/index.ts` nor any package export changes, so no production module reaches `actExplore` or `actOffers` yet.\n- `docs/kernel.md`. It records the one `judgeActBody` export under ruling 12dff549, and the condition that runtime and replay answers equal the pre-split corpus. `packages/derive/src/index.ts` already re-exports `judge.ts`, so that export is the only addition to the derive surface.\n- The oracle. `judge-oracle.setup.ts` wraps `judgeAct` and `judgeActForReplay` only inside the derive and replay Vitest configs. `scripts/e1-judge-oracle.mjs compare` checks 20 owners, 189 tests and 4,029 calls (1,932 runtime, 2,097 replay) against the frozen answers from baseline 270dc8c7.\n- The release identifiers. Only the Lending and Counter presentation digests change, because `expected.ts` is in their browser bundles. Ruling 0fcc2ec5 keeps both versions open through E1. Grow 2.0.0 is unchanged. `apps/room/page/assets.ts` is the producer's output.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main 17a917cd is an ancestor. The 57 artifacts name the head, and their paths equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only 17a917cd a4747ecf`. From 9abf2588 to the head only the note changes. From 00137c20 to 9abf2588 only the note and the two seal strings in `scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs` change.\n- Gate 1, END e62fa38d: FAIL, and it stays FAIL. `gate.log` has SHA-256 1aba4cc1…, names head 00137c20 and tree 7c23923e with 0 changed files, and fails only `security-entry-points.test.mjs:454` on the old `judge.ts` seal. Counting, Plain and Native did not run.\n- The follow-up under ruling 5fc60537, END db3519f1: PASS at 9abf2588. `result.json` has SHA-256 b3819cd0…, as the END states. Security passed all 12 cases, Counting 56, Plain 4851 with 1 skipped, and Native 1150 with 2 skipped. The natural selection was `moved`, from `judge.ts` and `program/guard-value.ts`, and all 16 moved cases passed in 9 modules. The Native wall time was 822.1 seconds. This is the first `moved` timing that GS-3 owed. The 612.2-second group sum is concurrent time, not wall time. With gate 1's Kernel and Node results, each stage has passed on source that differs only by the two seal strings.\n- The seal rows. They name `judge.ts` 3186b50c… and `client/expected.ts` d04561dd…, and the follow-up's Security owner accepted both. I judged the two production diffs themselves, above, not only the strings.\n- Page. END 2b9ee6cf records four local macOS steps passing at 9abf2588, with output in `2026-11-15-e1-page-browser-9abf2588a818-attempt1`. The Linux Page workflow has not run at this head. No Card filter matches.\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 79 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. One body of code now judges runtime, replay and exploration, as promise 9 requires. The split carries every exploration-only behavior in optional frame members. The frozen corpus of 4,029 runtime and replay calls shows that the recorded path did not move. Client exploration stays private until a later head exports it.\n\nSecurity: no finding. On `judge.ts`: a recorded act still passes every signature, actor, lifetime, grant-key and clock check, and its draft is unchanged in shape and order. `judgeActBody` cannot produce an entry, because its answer has no input. A rule that reaches the absent intent or an entry symbol stops judgment and emits no effects. On `expected.ts`: for the numeric items that the MCP server and the pages pass, the subject keys and revisions are unchanged, so no client signs a different `expected` than before. Three facts for the record:\n\n- The grant search now sets `clocked.clock` when it reads a grant's expiry. A recorded act already judges time, so its answer is unchanged. A keyless body uses the flag to answer `clock-behind`.\n- For a recorded act, `actBodyJudged` checks `item?.type !== act.on` twice in a row. The second check is redundant on that path. It costs nothing and changes no answer.\n- The 16 new `packages/client/src/act-*.ts` modules, about 1,500 lines, have no production caller at this head. `ScopeApi.explore` and its route stay held, as `docs/kernel.md` states.\n\nSimplification: no required change.\n\nKernel: E1 adds the one export `judgeActBody` under ruling 12dff549, and closes no K item. The note records the runtime and replay corpus comparison that the ruling requires.\n\nQualifications that stand: gate 1 is FAIL, and the follow-up is a partial run under 5fc60537. The browser steps ran on macOS only, and the Linux Page workflow is owed after publication. Scripted transport and recording limits stand. No hosted install or physical-room proof follows. This approval authorizes no push, landing, signing, installation, deployment or R integration. The planner decides the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4223ff6892a2cc755c5d79e731cc45b1394c5484","binding":"assigned","head":"a4747ecf8b4ff14ca393c9a10a777ee5b69dc01c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:360300a0965238cde41992d55a58aef4156ec2ac\"]","impl